# 1. RHEL lifecycle / platform ownership

## Main question

“Walk me through how you’d approach building a **RHEL 10 platform standard from scratch**. What does that process look like before a single server gets provisioned?”

## Clarifying bullets you can give if they need help

“You can think about things like:”

- Security and compliance baseline
- Standard build/image/template
- Required enterprise agents/tools
- Identity integration
- Patching/content lifecycle
- Documentation
- Pilot testing
- How you would move from lab to production readiness

## Follow-up questions

- “What would you include in the baseline standard?”
- “How would you validate that RHEL 10 is ready for enterprise use?”
- “Who would you need to involve before approving RHEL 10 for production?”
- “How would you handle standard software or security agents that are not yet supported on RHEL 10?”
- “What documentation would you produce for support/provisioning teams?”

## What you are listening for

Strong answer should sound like platform engineering, not just server administration. They should talk about standards, security, hardening, templates, automation, lifecycle, testing, documentation, and phased adoption.

## Red flag follow-up

If they only say, “I would install RHEL 10 and patch it,” ask:

“After the OS is installed, how do you make sure every future RHEL 10 server is built the same way and meets enterprise security standards?”

