# 2. Ansible automation depth

## Main question

“Your resume mentions building Ansible playbooks for configuration management and standardization. Can you describe one Ansible automation you personally designed? What made it idempotent, safe, and reusable?”

## Clarifying bullets you can give if they need help

“You can use any example: patching, baseline config, user management, package install, service config, security hardening, or monitoring setup.”

Ask them to cover:

- What problem it solved
- What the playbook changed
- How they tested it
- How they made sure it was safe to rerun
- How it handled different environments or OS versions

## Follow-up questions

- “Did you use Ansible modules or mostly shell commands?”
- “How did you prevent unnecessary changes on every run?”
- “How did you handle service restarts?”
- “Did you use handlers, templates, variables, roles, or conditionals?”
- “How did you test it before production?”
- “What happens if the playbook fails halfway through?”

## What you are listening for

Good answer should mention idempotency, modules, variables, handlers, testing, version control, check mode, lower environment testing, and safe rollback/backout thinking.

## Red flag follow-up

If they say they mostly use shell commands inside Ansible, ask:

“When would you use an Ansible module instead of a shell command, and why?”

A strong candidate should know that modules are usually safer, idempotent, and easier to validate.

