# 6. Vulnerability remediation

## Main question

“Your resume says you conducted vulnerability analysis and remediation. If a high-severity OpenSSL vulnerability is reported across thousands of Linux servers, how would you handle it?”

## Clarifying bullets you can give if they need help

“You can walk through the process from vulnerability notification to closure.”

They can cover:

- Confirm affected versions
- Identify impacted servers
- Check vendor advisory
- Confirm available fixed package
- Test patch
- Prioritize critical/internet-facing systems
- Schedule rollout
- Reboot or service restart needs
- Validate after remediation
- Report exceptions

## Follow-up questions

- “How would you know which servers are actually vulnerable?”
- “How would you confirm Red Hat has released the fix?”
- “How would you determine whether a reboot is needed?”
- “How would you prioritize the rollout?”
- “What would you do with servers that cannot be patched immediately?”
- “How would you prove remediation is complete?”

## What you are listening for

Strong answer should sound controlled and risk-aware. They should not blindly patch everything. They should identify impacted versions, test, coordinate, patch in phases, validate, and track exceptions.

## Red flag follow-up

If they say, “I would patch all servers immediately,” ask:

“How would you avoid causing an outage across production if the patch has an unexpected impact?”

