# 7. Engineering mindset / standards

## Main question

“This role is not mainly break/fix support. It is about defining Linux platform standards and enabling other teams. Can you give an example of something you turned from a manual operational process into a repeatable platform standard?”

## Clarifying bullets you can give if they need help

“It could be anything you standardized: server builds, patching, monitoring setup, security baseline, user access, storage setup, cloud image build, or documentation.”

Ask them to explain:

- What was manual before
- What problem it caused
- What standard they created
- What automation or documentation they built
- Who used it afterward
- How they measured success

## Follow-up questions

- “What made it a standard instead of just a one-time fix?”
- “How did other teams consume or use your work?”
- “Did you document it? What did the documentation include?”
- “How did you handle exceptions?”
- “How did you maintain the standard over time?”
- “What would you improve if you did it again?”

## What you are listening for

This is probably the most important culture-fit question. A strong candidate should talk about repeatability, documentation, ownership, automation, adoption, and long-term maintainability.

## Red flag follow-up

If they only talk about fixing tickets, ask:

“What did you do to prevent that same issue from coming back again across other servers?”

